course: everyday
lesson: 2
title: Balance and falling well
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: You stay balanced when the point where your weight is centred (your centre of gravity) is above the area between your feet.
explain: Spreading your feet and bending your knees makes that area wider and your centre lower, so you are much harder to knock over.
explain: Your inner ear, your eyes and the feeling in your feet and muscles tell your brain how you are standing, and it corrects you all the time.
explain: If you do fall, the trick is to spread the bump over a big soft part of your body and over a little more time: tuck your chin, bend your knees and elbows, and roll onto your side or bottom.
explain: Catching yourself on one straight, stiff arm puts all the force into a small wrist, which is why broken wrists are so common; judo students practise falling for this reason.
explain: A girl in Leeds tripped while running and threw her hand straight out to stop herself; all her weight landed on one stiff wrist and it broke.
explain: A boy in Texas stood on a wobbly chair on top of a table to reach a shelf; the chair tipped, he fell onto the hard floor and needed stitches in his chin.
fact: You keep your balance when your centre of gravity is above the area between your feet.
fact: Standing with your feet apart gives you a wider base and better balance.
fact: Bending your knees lowers your centre of gravity and makes you steadier.
fact: The inner ear has a balance organ that senses how your head moves and tilts.
fact: Spinning round fast confuses the balance organ in your inner ear, and that is why you feel dizzy afterwards.
fact: A tightrope walker carries a long pole because it helps her keep her centre of gravity over the rope.
fact: Your eyes help you balance, so balancing is harder with your eyes closed.
fact: Falling onto one straight, stiff arm often breaks the wrist.
fact: Tucking your chin to your chest helps keep your head from hitting the ground.
fact: Rolling as you land spreads the force over a bigger part of your body.
fact: Landing on soft, padded parts like your bottom, thigh and side hurts less than landing on bones.
fact: Bending your knees when you land from a jump makes the landing softer.
fact: Judo and other martial arts teach students how to fall safely.
fact: After a bang on the head, feeling sick, dizzy or very sleepy is a warning sign: tell an adult at once, because you may need a doctor.
fact: Putting a straight, stiff arm out to stop a fall is one of the most common ways children break a wrist.
fact: Standing on chairs, buckets or furniture that wobble causes many falls at home, so use a proper step stool.
exercise: Why is it easier to balance with your feet apart? | Your base is wider | Your feet get warmer | Your legs become lighter | Your inner ear works faster
exercise: How does bending your knees help you stay standing on a moving bus? | It lowers your centre of gravity | It makes the bus go slower | It makes your shoes grip like glue | It makes you lighter
exercise: Which part of the body has a balance organ? | The inner ear | The stomach lining | The tongue | The elbow
exercise: Why is it harder to stand on one leg with your eyes closed? | Your eyes help your brain keep balance | Closing your eyes makes your body heavier | Your ears stop working in the dark | Your leg muscles fall asleep
exercise: When you start to fall, what should you do with your head? | Tuck your chin to your chest | Throw your head back | Turn your head to look behind | Hold your head up high and stiff
exercise: Why is it bad to catch a fall on one stiff, straight arm? | All the force goes into the small wrist | Your arm will get dirty | Straight arms make you fall much faster to the ground | It is only bad on grass
exercise: How does rolling help when you fall? | It spreads the force over more of your body | It makes the ground softer | It stops gravity for a moment | It makes you fall more slowly through the air
exercise: Which parts of the body are best to land on in a fall? | Soft, padded parts like the bottom | The knees and the elbows, which are hard | The head and the neck | One hand with a stiff arm
exercise: How should you land after jumping down from a low wall? | On both feet with bent knees | On stiff, straight legs | On your heels with locked knees | On one foot only
exercise: Why does bending your knees make a landing softer? | You stop more slowly, so the jolt is smaller | Bent knees make your whole body lighter for a moment | It makes the ground bounce | It makes you fall more slowly
exercise: Where do students practise falling safely? | In judo and other martial arts | Only in swimming lessons | Nowhere, falling cannot be learned | Only in ballet
exercise: What tells your brain how your head is tilting? | The balance organ in your inner ear | Your hair | Your teeth | The muscles in your fingertips and toes
exercise: Why does a tightrope walker often hold a long pole? | It helps keep her centre of gravity over the rope | The pole makes her much lighter on the rope | It is only for decoration | It pulls her up towards the sky, like a kite would
exercise: A surfer crouches low on the board. Why? | A lower centre of gravity keeps her steady | It makes the wave bigger | It keeps her hair dry | It makes the board go faster through the water than standing
test: A boy in Leeds trips on a pavement and feels himself falling forward. What is the safest way to land? | Bend his arms and roll onto his side | Throw out one stiff arm | Keep his body straight like a plank | Land on his chin
test: Which stance is hardest to push over? | Feet apart and knees bent | Feet together and legs straight | Standing on tiptoe | Standing on one foot
test: Mia spins around fast and then feels dizzy. Which part of her body was confused? | The balance organ in her inner ear | Her stomach, which sloshes as she spins round | Her knees | Her fingers
test: What would happen if you jumped off a step and landed with locked knees? | The jolt would go hard through your legs | You would bounce back up | You would land more softly | Nothing at all would be different from bent knees
test: Why do skiers and skaters learn how to fall before anything else? | Falling well prevents injuries when they fall | Falling is the fastest way down | Falling makes the snow softer | It is a rule with no reason
test: Which is true about broken wrists? | They often come from catching a fall on a stiff arm | They only happen to adults | They come from walking too fast | They happen only in winter, when ice is on the ground
test: Why is it easier to balance on a bus if you stand sideways with feet apart? | Your base is wider in the direction the bus jolts | Standing sideways makes the bus smoother | Your inner ear works better sideways | It makes you heavier on the floor
test: A cat falls and lands with its legs bent. How do the bent legs help? | They slow the stop and soften the landing | They make the cat lighter | They make the ground softer | They let the cat fall more slowly through the air to the ground
test: Why does tucking your chin help when you fall backwards? | It keeps the back of your head off the ground | It makes you fall more slowly so you land gently | It keeps your mouth closed | It helps you see the sky
test: about to fall off the wall, stick my arm out straight?? | No, tuck in and roll onto a soft side | Yes, a straight arm stops you best | Yes, and lock your elbow tight | Yes, land on both straight arms
test: bus wobbling, how do i not fall over standing | Feet apart, knees a bit bent | Feet together, legs stiff | Stand on tiptoe | Close your eyes and hold still
test: why's it so hard standing on 1 leg eyes shut | Your eyes help you balance | Your ears stop working then | Your legs get weaker in the dark | The floor feels further away
test: jumping off the step, how do i land so it dont hurt | Bend your knees as you land | Land with straight stiff legs | Land on your heels with locked knees | Land on one straight arm
test: fell off the wall and banged my head, feel sick and dizzy now | Tell an adult right away, you may need a doctor | Lie down and sleep it off alone in your room | It is fine if there is no blood | Have a snack and keep climbing
test: gonna stand on the swivel chair to get the biscuits, ok? | No, it can roll or spin and you will fall | Yes, if you hold the back of it | Yes, if you are quick about it | Yes, if somebody holds it and spins it very slowly
test: Lily reckons u should always land on your hands when u fall. right? | No, bend, tuck and roll to spread the hit | Yes, hands are made for landing on | Yes, keep your arms as stiff as you can | Yes, and lock your elbows too
test: omg Leo fell off the wall and put his arm out and it snapped clean in half, my mum said | The real lesson is not to land on a stiff straight arm; «in half» is probably exaggerated | The real lesson is never to use your arms at all when you move | It proves walls are only dangerous for boys | Every detail is surely true since a mum said it
test: someone in year 5 reckons if you fall with your eyes shut you can't get hurt cos you don't tense up lol | That is a rumour; eyes help balance, and you fall safer by tucking in and rolling | It is true, closing your eyes makes every fall harmless | It is true, but only when you fall backwards | It is true for grown-ups but not for children
test: Ava wobbled on a chair and fell and everyone laughed, should i tell the other class | No, there is nothing to learn from it, and it would hurt her | Yes, because it is funny and it is true | Yes, so they laugh at her too and she learns to be careful next time on chairs | Yes, but only if you make it sound worse
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the condition under which a standing person retains balance? | Balance is retained whilst the centre of gravity remains above the area enclosed by the feet | Balance is retained solely whilst the eyes remain closed | Balance is retained only whilst the feet are placed tightly together | Balance is retained whenever the arms are held rigidly at the sides
exam: Identify the principal hazard associated with the extension of a single straight and rigid arm for the purpose of arresting a fall. | It frequently results in the fracture of the wrist | It invariably results in an injury to the ankle | It causes the person to fall with greater speed | It presents no hazard whatsoever to children
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the lowering of the centre of gravity by the bending of the knees. | It renders the person steadier and less liable to fall | It renders the person less steady and more liable to fall | It has no effect of any description upon balance | It is of benefit solely to persons standing on ice
exam: It is given that a person desires to reach an elevated shelf at home. Determine the most appropriate means of doing so. | The employment of a proper and stable step stool | The standing upon a revolving office chair | The standing upon an upturned bucket | The balancing upon the arm of a sofa
